## Decoding the question
**"Do you think"** asks for your **opinion**, argued with reasons on both sides. The question has two scopes: **"all spheres of the public domain"** (bureaucracy, judiciary, business, media) and **"especially politics"** — so give politics most space. Explain the saying "**women cover (hold up) half the sky**" in the introduction.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) The saying
"**Women hold up half the sky**", a saying associated with **Mao Zedong**, means that women contribute **equally** to society and should therefore share equally in its decisions.

#### ii) The argument
I agree that women should have an **equal share** of public life, especially politics — justice, democracy and better policy all demand it. But equal numbers should be treated as a **goal reached through equal opportunity**, with **quotas as a bridge**, not as a rigid formula imposed overnight. Numbers must also come with **real power**.

### 2. The meaning of the saying
Women do half — often more than half — of society's work, paid and unpaid. If they "hold up half the sky", excluding them from decisions is both **unjust** and **wasteful**. The idea underlies **SDG target 5.5**, which calls for women's full and effective participation and **equal opportunities for leadership** at all levels. The Beijing Platform for Action (==1995==), Critical Area G, likewise called for **gender balance** in power and decision-making.

### 3. Women in Pakistan's public domain today
Women are about **half** of the population but roughly **one-fifth** of the National Assembly, mostly on **60 reserved seats**; only **11** won general seats in ==2024==. In ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers**. Women hold a **10%** quota in federal jobs but few senior posts, and remain a tiny share of the police; **Justice Ayesha Malik** became the first woman Supreme Court judge only in ==2022==.

::: fig pyramid | Women's presence narrows at each level of public life in Pakistan.
Top leadership: no women federal ministers (2025); one woman chief minister (2024)
Senior posts: few women in top civil-service grades, high courts and boardrooms
Legislatures: about one-fifth of the National Assembly, mostly reserved seats
Voters: 46.1% of registered voters; turnout 43% vs 52% for men
Citizens: women are about half of the population
:::

### 4. For parity 1: justice and democracy
Political equality is a **human right** — **UDHR** Article 21, **CEDAW** Article 7, Pakistan's **Articles 25 and 34**. Quaid-e-Azam **Muhammad Ali Jinnah** told students at Aligarh in ==1944== that no nation can rise to the height of glory unless its women are side by side with its men. A democracy in which half the citizens hold a fifth of the seats is not fully representative. **Hanna Pitkin**'s idea of **descriptive** representation holds that legislatures should broadly resemble the people.

### 5. For parity 2: interests and better decisions

#### i) Interests
Women's experiences — of care, maternity, violence and work — differ from men's. **Anne Phillips** (==1995==) argued that these issues reach the agenda only when women are **present**.

#### ii) Better decisions
Diverse bodies consider a wider range of problems and solutions, and are less prone to "group-think"; research on company boards and committees points the same way.

#### iii) Evidence
Pakistan's women legislators drove the **harassment** law (==2010==) and the **honour-killing** law (==2016==); in India, women village leaders invested more in **drinking water**.

### 6. For parity 3: role models and proof that it works

#### i) Role models
Women in office change ideas of who can lead and raise girls' aspirations.

#### ii) Critical mass
Below about **30%**, women remain **tokens** (Kanter, ==1977==).

#### iii) Parity in practice
**France** adopted a **parity law** in ==2000==; **Rwanda**'s parliament is over **60%** women; **Nepal**'s 2015 constitution requires one-third, and women won about **35%** of its House in ==2026==.

#### iv) In simple words
If half the passengers on a bus are women but only men are allowed to drive, decide the route and set the fares, the journey will be planned around men's needs. Parity simply means that women, who are half the passengers, also share the steering wheel. It does not push men out of the driver's seat; it ends the unwritten rule that only men may sit there. Shared driving makes for a safer journey for everyone.

### 7. Against rigid parity 1: merit and choice
Critics argue that posts should go to the most able, whatever their sex, and that a fixed 50% rule may promote less qualified people. Some women may **choose** not to enter politics because of care duties or personal preference; forcing equal numbers ignores choice. A rigid 50% rule could also be hard to fill in some fields and regions in the short term, leaving seats empty or filled by unprepared nominees.

### 8. Against rigid parity 2: women are not one group
Women differ by **class, religion, region and party**. A woman legislator from a landed family may not represent poor rural women. Where parties control selection, forced parity may produce **proxies** — wives and daughters of male politicians — rather than independent women. Numbers alone, without independence, would give the appearance of equality without its substance.

### 9. Politics — why it matters most
Politics is the sphere where **laws, budgets and priorities** are set for all other spheres, and where the state's commitment to equality is either honoured or ignored. Women's presence in politics shapes education, health, employment and justice. That is why the question says "**especially**" politics, and why quotas are most widely used there. Pakistan's own experience shows the difference: the laws on harassment, acid attacks, anti-women practices and honour killing came only after women's numbers rose to about a fifth of parliament.

### 10. Other spheres of the public domain
- **Bureaucracy:** the 10% job quota is unfilled at senior levels; women are needed in district administration and police.
- **Judiciary:** women judges improve access to justice for women in family and violence cases, where many women hesitate to speak before men.
- **Business and boards:** listed companies must have at least one woman director; Norway's **40%** board quota (==2003==) shows stronger options.
- **Media and academia:** women shape public debate and knowledge.

### 11. My position

#### i) Equal opportunity first
Remove barriers — party gatekeeping, money, harassment, lack of childcare and mobility.

#### ii) Quotas as a bridge
Use quotas to reach a **critical mass** (at least 33%) and move towards parity, as Nepal has.

#### iii) Power, not just numbers
Women must hold **ministries, committee chairs and senior posts**, and be **directly elected**, so that equal numbers mean equal influence. Diversity among women — rural, minority and working-class women — should also be sought, so that parity represents all women, not only the elite.

### 12. Conclusion
If women hold up half the sky, they should share half the decisions. Equal participation in all public spheres — above all in politics — is the right goal, demanded by justice and supported by evidence. Pakistan, with women at about a fifth of parliament and absent from the 2025 cabinet, is far from it. The way forward is **equal opportunity**, with **quotas as a bridge** and **real power** as the test.

## Why this answer scores
- Explains the **saying** and links it to **SDG 5.5**.
- Weighs **both sides** and gives a clear, reasoned **opinion**.
- Covers **"all spheres"** but gives **politics** most weight, as asked.
- A **pyramid figure** shows how women's presence narrows at each level.
